data: {"step": "response", "status": "streaming", "detail": "For the officer joining this caseload, here is a concise orientation note:  \n\n**Case Distribution**: Cases are concentrated in **\u0641\u06cc\u0635\u0644 \u0622\u0628\u0627\u062f** (Faisalabad: 19 cases), **\u0644\u0627\u06c1\u0648\u0631** (Lahore: 18 cases), and **\u0631\u0627\u0648\u0644\u067e\u0646\u0688\u06cc** (Rawalpindi: 10 cases). Other districts, including **\u0627\u0633\u0644\u0627\u0645 \u0622\u0628\u0627\u062f** (Islamabad), **\u0686\u0646\u06cc\u0648\u0679** (Chiniot), **\u062d\u06cc\u062f\u0631 \u0622\u0628\u0627\u062f** (Hyderabad), **\u06a9\u0631\u0627\u0686\u06cc \u0627\u06cc\u0633\u0679** (Karachi East), **\u06a9\u0631\u0627\u0686\u06cc \u0648\u0633\u0637\u06cc** (Karachi Central), and **\u0645\u0644\u062a\u0627\u0646** (Multan), have 5\u20131 cases each [Document 1].  \n\n**Case Mix**: Current cases (2026) show a **surge in Pakistan Penal Code (PPC)** cases (39) compared to 2024 (13), alongside a decline in **Arms Ordinance 1965** cases (16 vs. 13). New categories include **CNSA 1997** (12), **PECA 2016** (9), **Punjab Domestic Violence Act** (4), and **Illegal Dispossession Act 2005** (2), reflecting diversification into domestic violence, economic crimes, and land disputes [Document 2].  \n\n**Arrests**: Only **11 of 73 FIRs** record at least one arrest; **62 FIRs** note no arrests (due to ongoing investigations, absconding, or explicit non-arrest notes) [Document 3].  \n\n**Reporting Delays**: The mean time from incident to report is **1401.3 minutes (~23.4 hours)** in 2026 (across 51 FIRs), a stark increase from **15.0 minutes** in 2024 [Document 4].  \n\n**Weapon Licensing**: Of 32 weapon-register entries, **30 recovered weapons** have **no licence recorded**, and **2 entries** lack licence status entirely, leaving compliance unconfirmed [Document 5].  \n\nOfficers should prioritize districts with higher case volumes, prepare for low arrest rates, and note delays in reporting. Weapon-related cases under Arms Ordinance 1965 and PPC require attention to unlicensed weapons and evolving legal frameworks."}

